-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.14 (2026-11-21)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * Rows 629/802: when a start cannot be recorded because the person set GRUB_GFXPAYLOAD_LINUX=text (Debian's 10_linux
    then writes no load_video into its entries), the problem says so in a sentence of its own (LOCAL102, ten
    languages): the file and the line that set it (/etc/default/grub, or a file of /etc/default/grub.d, the last one
    grub-mkconfig reads), that GRUB's menu cannot show by itself after a start that did not finish with this setting,
    and what brings it back (remove or change that line, then sudo update-grub). It no longer offers "repair", which
    runs update-grub again and cannot help while the setting stays. LOCAL97 stays for Debian's 10_linux or 00_header
    in a form the package does not know.
  * The postinst writes the menu again at an upgrade from 0.1.13 too, so the problem is said in its new words at once.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.13 (2026-11-21)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * Rows 629/802: grub2-common's /etc/grub.d/10_linux is no longer diverted (Debian Policy, appendix 7: "Do not
    attempt to divert a conffile"). A Linux entry records its start through the load_video line Debian's 10_linux
    writes into it: 01_sparky_stereo_menu writes 00_header's own load_video again under another name and a load_video
    that first calls recordfail when GRUB's "chosen" is an entry of this system's Linux (gnulinux-...), never one of
    another system. The menu after a start that did not finish, the forced check, the reason entry and the note at
    login are as in 0.1.11. With GRUB_GFXPAYLOAD_LINUX=text Debian writes no load_video: 90_sparky_stereo_start says
    and keeps that a start cannot be recorded (LOCAL97), as the former wrapper did for an entry it did not know.
  * The postinst gives Debian's 10_linux back at its name, with the person's changes to it (a preinst removal would
    make dpkg delete it as the link 0.1.12 shipped there), then writes the menu again with update-grub as its last step.
  * initramfs-tools' update-initramfs.conf stays diverted (initramfs-tools reads one fixed file), and no longer costs
    initramfs-tools its conffile: when initramfs-tools was unpacked earlier in the same dpkg run, the preinst moves its
    pending update-initramfs.conf.dpkg-new to the diverted name before our link is unpacked (dpkg recorded the
    conffile "newconffile" and never installed it). The postrm does the reverse.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.12 (2026-11-17)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * The splash of a restart or a shutdown shows the main screen as the person set it now (ORDER 639; Daniel, 2026-11-17:
    "the tv is switching to 2d before restart - why?"). Plymouth's shutdown units start plymouthd with /proc/cmdline,
    the choice this boot started with; a television made the main screen, or put in 3D, after the start was not named
    in it, and Plymouth gives a screen it is not told about its preferred mode, so the television fell to 2D at the
    restart. plymouth-reboot, -poweroff, -halt and -kexec.service now start plymouthd through
    sparky-stereo-boot plymouth-shutdown, which hands it the plymouth.stereo= exported last
    (/var/lib/sparky-stereo-boot/main-output.json, written at every export) with plymouthd's own --kernel-command-line;
    the journal says when that differs from the start's, and why the start's stays when the record cannot be read
  * Recommends plymouth 26.134.222-1+stereo3d4 and sparky-stereo-plymouth 20261117: one eye on every screen the splash
    does not convert, and the splash laid out again for a screen that comes later
  * tests/shutdown-splash-tests.py: the line plymouthd is given in each case

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.11 (2026-11-15)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * After a start that did not finish, GRUB's menu shows by itself at the next start, with the older kernel under
    "Advanced options", and the file systems are checked at that start (ORDER 572b; Daniel, 2026-11-15: "I agree with
    your idea after a failed start, alongside the check filesystem"). Every start that follows a finished one keeps the
    hidden menu and no wait (Daniel, 2026-10-07: "users don't sit waiting"). 0.1.10 measured that a hidden menu with no
    wait opens with no key, so a newest kernel that no longer started left no way to the older one
  * The mechanism is GRUB's own recordfail, which Debian's grub2 carries switched off: debian/patches/quick-boot.patch
    (Colin Watson, with Richard Laager and Robie Basak) writes the function in 00_header and the call in 10_linux only
    when GRUB was configured with --enable-quick-boot, which debian/rules gives Ubuntu's build alone; 00_header's test
    of the flag and grub2-common.service, which clears it after systemd's boot-complete.target and after a resume, are
    there on Debian as they are. So 01_sparky_stereo_menu writes the function, and /etc/grub.d/10_linux becomes a link
    to a program that runs Debian's 10_linux (moved aside with dpkg-divert to /etc/sparky-stereo-boot/10_linux.debian)
    and writes the call after each "menuentry ... {" of its output; nothing else of Debian's output changes
  * The time the menu waits is GRUB's GRUB_RECORDFAIL_TIMEOUT when the person set it; otherwise a menu that is shown
    anyway (another system on the computer, the person's own timeout) keeps its time, and a hidden one is shown for 30
    seconds, GRUB's default for it
  * The menu says why it is shown: its last entry, there only while the flag is set, has the reason as its title in
    the system's language and shows the longer text when chosen (/etc/grub.d/90_sparky_stereo_start)
  * The check: GRUB puts fsck.mode=force on the kernel line of that start, in every Linux entry (systemd-fsck(8):
    "force unconditionally results in full file system checks"; initramfs-tools checks the root file system with it).
    btrfs and xfs check themselves when mounted (fsck.btrfs(8), fsck.xfs(8) do nothing) and are named as such
  * At login the person is told that the previous start did not finish, that the menu was shown, and what the check
    found for each file system (no errors, errors repaired, errors that remain with what to do), through the
    edition's one engine; the note goes at the start after that
  * A start has finished when systemd reaches boot-complete.target (systemd.special(7)), as Debian's
    grub2-common.service has it; sparky-stereo-boot-login.service, ordered before that target and after
    display-manager.service, adds the login screen: a start whose login screen failed to start has not finished
  * Where GRUB cannot write its environment block (Debian's own check_writable: LVM or RAID under /boot, btrfs, zfs),
    the flag is kept in a block on the EFI system partition, which GRUB can write, and update-grub says so; on a BIOS
    start there is no such place, update-grub says that the menu cannot show by itself there, and the way to always
    show it (SPARKY_STEREO_MENU=shown)
  * Under Secure Boot, "Advanced options" opens again. The check of 0.1.3 that asks shim about the newest kernel when
    GRUB reads the menu (linux KERNEL) left that kernel loaded, and GRUB starts whatever is loaded when Enter is
    pressed on a submenu (grub-core/normal/menu.c, grub_menu_execute_entry: "Implicit execution of boot, only if
    something is loaded", before the submenu is shown): Enter on "Advanced options" started the newest kernel with no
    boot image, which stopped in a kernel panic, and the older kernel could not be reached. Found in the rig. When the
    menu will be shown, the kernel is now unloaded after the check: GRUB's EFI loader is given MokManager, a file shim
    accepts and no kernel (grub-core/loader/efi/linux.c unloads right after the file opens)
  * SPARKY_STEREO_UNFINISHED_START=no in /etc/default/sparky-stereo-boot turns all of it off; sparky-stereo-boot status
    says where a start is recorded
  * tests/unfinished-start-tests.sh: 84 cases (the rule with a stand-in for grub-probe, what the menu's files write
    checked by grub-script-check, the login check, the check's verdicts, the ten languages)

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.10 (2026-11-14)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * GRUB's menu is hidden, with no wait, when this is the only system on the computer, and shown for 5 seconds when
    another system is found (ORDER 572; Daniel, 2026-10-07: "dual boot found at install: os-prober on and menu shown;
    single OS: os-prober off, menu hidden", "users don't sit waiting"; 2026-11-14, after an install on a notebook
    showed the menu for 5 seconds: "hidden grub, specially when there's only us installed", "if it has windows ok").
    30-sparky-stereo-boot.cfg sets GRUB_TIMEOUT_STYLE and GRUB_TIMEOUT at every update-grub: os-prober, when the
    installer (or the person) turned it on, says whether another system is there; an os-prober that fails shows the
    menu and says so. The person's own GRUB_TIMEOUT_STYLE, GRUB_HIDDEN_TIMEOUT, or a GRUB_TIMEOUT other than Debian's 5
    in /etc/default/grub are kept; SPARKY_STEREO_MENU=shown always shows the menu
  * sudo sparky-stereo-boot menu shows a hidden menu once, at the next start, for 30 seconds; the boot does the same
    by itself when a Secure Boot key waits for its enrolment. Keys are not a way in: with no wait GRUB looks at the
    keyboard once (in QEMU, Shift held and Esc pressed through the start opened nothing under SeaBIOS and OVMF)
  * An upgrade of a system installed before this rule looks once for another system (os-prober) and, when it finds
    one, writes the file the installer writes (/etc/default/grub.d/sparky-stereo-dual-boot.cfg); Depends on os-prober
  * The menu is never drawn without a loaded font. Under Secure Boot, Debian's signed GRUB refuses every font file
    outside its own image (GRUB 2.14 grub-core/kern/efi/sb.c: a file type the verifier does not list is "prohibited
    by secure boot policy"; proved: loadfont of the edition's font answers exactly that), and Debian's 00_header starts
    the graphical terminal whether its font loaded or not, so the menu had 16 pixel ASCII glyphs, a "?" in a box for the
    arrows, the frame and every accented letter, and English text. Now update-grub under Secure Boot names no font
    file: GRUB loads the font bundled in its signed image (debian/build-efi-images puts unicode.pf2 in its memdisk;
    grub-core/font/font.c looks there first), and since that font has one size, 16 pixels, the menu is shown at the
    resolution where it has the menu's text height on this screen (the nearest of the screen's own shape, then
    1024x768, then the firmware's). SPARKY_STEREO_SECUREBOOT_TEXT=native keeps the screen's resolution. A screen on
    which 16 pixels already is that height keeps its resolution
  * /etc/grub.d/01_sparky_stereo_menu, right after 00_header: when GRUB runs under shim's lock or the font file is
    gone, GRUB loads its own font, takes that resolution and starts the graphical terminal again; with no font at
    all the menu is the firmware's text console. This covers Secure Boot switched on after the menu was written and
    the person's own GRUB_FONT; the boot check (sparky-stereo-boot-check.service) writes the menu again when Secure
    Boot is not what it was at the last update-grub
  * The screen's size comes from both EDID fields: the screen size field (VESA E-EDID Release A2, 3.6.2: "The host
    (source) is expected to use this data to get a rough idea of the image size to generate properly scaled text and
    icons"), and the first detailed timing's image size (3.10.2, millimetres) for the millimetres when the two agree;
    when they differ the screen size field decides and update-grub says so. A notebook panel declared 29 x 17 cm
    there and 344 x 193 mm in its timing: its menu text was sized for a 15.5 inch panel on a 13.3 inch one
  * When shim refuses the newest kernel the enrolment entry is still the default, and a hidden menu is shown for it
    (10 seconds), so the person sees it selected, as before
  * The file's own exported values, met again when it is sourced inside a process that inherited them, are worked out
    again and no longer recorded as the person's
  * tests/grub-hook-tests.sh: 41 cases (the two sizes, Secure Boot, the menu rule, the person's settings)

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.9 (2026-11-09)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * The boot splash follows the person's depth limits (ORDER 481; Daniel, 2026-11-05: "read the depth config by, and in this
    order of importance: 1 - User choice (but based on 2); 2 - Current display"). Each stereo output of plymouth.stereo=
    ends with popped=PIXELS:sunk=PIXELS, the main screen's limits as Stereo KWin resolves them: the person's PoppedLimit
    and SunkLimit (kwinrc [StereoDepth], the system's /etc/xdg/kwinrc under the person's own file, as KConfig reads them)
    times the display's factor from libstereo-policy (the kind from the mode's flags, the size from the EDID, as KWin asks
    it), pixels of an eye 1920 wide. A limit nobody saved is the display's default; a limit of 0 stays 0
  * The socket service reads the kwinrc of the account that connected (the peer's uid is checked as before), not data the
    client sends; the session's path unit also watches ~/.config/kwinrc, so a change in 3D Depth reaches the next boot
  * No silent fallbacks: no limits saved is said in the journal and shown by status ("the display's default"); a saved
    value that is no limit (not a number, below 0, above 1000) or a kwinrc that cannot be read is kept as a problem
    (splash-depth), and the display's default boots; a libstereo-policy that cannot answer is kept as a problem
    (splash-depth-display), and the person's limits are used as they are
  * Depends on libstereo-policy1 (>= 1.15.0); `sparky-stereo-boot export-file FILE USER [KWINRC]` takes the kwinrc
    for tests
  * tests/run-tests.py: ten cases for the limits (nothing saved, both saved, one saved, 0, a television, red/cyan, a
    headset twin, the system file under the person's, two kinds of value that are no limit), each against libstereo-policy's
    rule written out in the test

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.8 (2026-11-03)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * This computer's DKMS key follows the edition key's design (ORDER 374; ORDER 260, Daniel, 2026-10-25: "we never
    know when the user will migrate the mb or even have to reset it"): DKMS signs the modules it builds on this
    computer (NVIDIA's open modules among them) with this computer's own key, which Secure Boot accepts only once it
    is in the MOK list, and no package enrolls it (Debian #1076269; dkms-project/dkms #429, closed as stale). Its
    certificate (dkms's mok_certificate, /var/lib/dkms/mok.pub by default; DER, a PEM one converted) is kept next to
    every shim as dkms-this-computer.der, and removed there when the computer has no DKMS key, by the postinst, the
    kernel hook, the shim and GRUB triggers and repair, as the edition's is
  * GRUB's menu has a second entry, "Secure Boot: enroll this computer's DKMS key", in the system's language with the
    steps in the nine languages, starting MokManager as the first does ("Enroll key from disk" asks only for a
    confirmation). GRUB looks for the certificate on the partition, by its UUID, when it reads the menu, so the entry
    appears as soon as DKMS has made the key, with no update-grub; update-grub runs once on the upgrade, for the entry
  * sparky-stereo-boot-dkms-key.path places the certificate when DKMS makes or changes the key (the installed
    system's first boot signs the modules its image built unsigned, dkms 3.4.4-1+sparky4), and checks the
    enrolment again when the running kernel's modules.dep is written (depmod, after DKMS installs the modules it
    signed), through sparky-stereo-boot-secureboot.service, which also runs at every boot
  * Under Secure Boot, a DKMS key that is not in shim's MOK list while DKMS modules signed with it are installed for
    the running kernel is said and kept, naming the GRUB entry that enrolls it (no silent fallbacks, Daniel,
    2026-11-01: "either it works or not and the user must know"); it clears at the first boot after the enrolment
  * A DKMS certificate that cannot be written or read is said and kept (esp-dkms-certificate), with
    secureboot.py esp as its repair; the purge removes dkms-this-computer.der from the partition too
